      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p1-divergent",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? The current date is early October 2026, in the Cubs' offseason (their 2026 season ended in the Wild Card Series). A prior experiment found that triaging the November minor-league free-agent pool offers little: the low-attention pool holds under one next-season contributor per club per year, and a simple 'played in MLB last year' rule already finds most value. Prefer recurring decisions where the value at stake is large enough to matter, where public information available at decision time could plausibly beat a simple rule, and where a retrospective test on public history is possible. Consider front office, player development, scouting, coaching and business-of-baseball decisions, and name where software is unnecessary or clubs are already ahead. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names one problem, the decision owner, the concrete recurring decision and its timing, the size of value at stake, the mechanism of advantage, the simple baseline a club already uses, and the software intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Problem=spring-training signal underuse; owner=GM and manager; decision=final Opening Day roster spots and early-season role assignments each late March; value=a few marginal roster slots and leverage roles over the first weeks; mechanism=spring Statcast-tracked velocity, spin and exit-velocity changes may carry real signal even though spring outcome stats are noise; baseline=ignore spring stats and rely on prior-season performance plus contract status; intervention=a model that combines spring physical-tracking deltas with prior-season projections, backtested on public spring tracking against regular-season results.\nASSUMPTION: Public spring-training tracking data covers enough parks, players and seasons to support a retrospective test.\nASSUMPTION: Clubs already use internal spring tracking at a much finer grain, so a public-data edge may be small or zero.\nASSUMPTION: Spring velocity changes partly reflect deliberate ramp-up schedules, which could mask any real signal.\nUNKNOWN: spring_tracking_public_coverage_by_season\nUNKNOWN: spring_velocity_delta_predictive_validity\n\nIDEA: Problem=postseason roster construction for short series; owner=manager, GM and analytics staff; decision=choosing the 26-man roster per round in October, including the count of pitchers versus position players and specialist slots such as pinch runner or third catcher; value=high-leverage innings concentrated in very few games; mechanism=simulating series-specific opponent handedness, off days and starter usage may reveal that the marginal 13th pitcher is worth less than a bench bat or runner; baseline=carry the regular-season structure with the top pitchers; intervention=a series simulator that scores roster variants using public schedules, opponent splits and historical postseason usage.\nASSUMPTION: Postseason sample sizes are too small for direct outcome backtesting, so validation would depend on simulator calibration rather than observed wins.\nASSUMPTION: The edge applies only in seasons when the Cubs reach the postseason, which makes this an episodic decision rather than a reliably recurring one.\nASSUMPTION: Contending clubs likely already run this kind of simulation internally.\nUNKNOWN: marginal_postseason_roster_slot_value\nUNKNOWN: historical_postseason_innings_by_roster_slot\n\nIDEA: Problem=international amateur bonus-pool allocation; owner=international scouting director; decision=distributing the annual signing pool across prospects at the January signing period, with verbal agreements made years earlier; value=the full annual pool plus long-tail prospect value; mechanism=public prospect rankings and published bonuses could show whether value per dollar is higher in concentrated top signings or in spread mid-tier signings; baseline=commit to a few top-ranked players early; intervention=a portfolio model trained on public signing-class bonuses and eventual prospect-list or MLB outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: The real constraint is early verbal agreements with trainers, not analysis, so software may not change behavior.\nASSUMPTION: Outcome lag of six or more years leaves few labeled classes, and rules have changed across eras.\nASSUMPTION: Public bonus data is incomplete for lower-bonus signees, which biases any test toward top signings.\nUNKNOWN: public_bonus_coverage_below_top_signees\nUNKNOWN: international_class_outcome_label_availability\n\nIDEA: Problem=coaching-staff hiring evaluation; owner=president of baseball operations and GM; decision=filling hitting, pitching and bench coach vacancies, typically in October and November; value=staff influence on many players at once, though hard to isolate; mechanism=public player-level changes before and after a coach's arrival at prior stops, adjusted for aging and regression, could separate coaches with repeatable effects from those who benefited from roster churn; baseline=reputation, interviews and network referrals; intervention=a coach-attribution model on public staff histories and player performance deltas.\nASSUMPTION: Coach effects may be unidentifiable because coaches move alongside front-office philosophies and roster changes.\nASSUMPTION: Public staff-tenure records are complete enough to link coaches to player cohorts.\nASSUMPTION: This may overlap with already explored attribution families and should be checked for duplication.\nUNKNOWN: coach_effect_identifiability\nUNKNOWN: public_coaching_staff_history_completeness\n\nIDEA: Problem=early-season weather and postponement exposure at Wrigley; owner=business operations together with the league; decision=managing April and May home-date risk, including ticket and promotion plans and pitcher scheduling around likely postponements; value=lost gate revenue, doubleheader workload and staff-planning costs; mechanism=public historical weather and postponement records could quantify risk by date to inform promotion placement and rotation contingency planning; baseline=static calendar planning; intervention=a date-risk calendar and contingency planner.\nASSUMPTION: Schedule and postponement authority sits largely with the league, which limits club leverage.\nASSUMPTION: The business side likely already uses commercial weather services, so software may be unnecessary here.\nUNKNOWN: club_control_over_postponement_decisions\nUNKNOWN: historical_wrigley_postponement_counts\n\nIDEA: Problem=where software is unnecessary; owner=analytics leadership; decision=annual triage of which tooling to build; value=staff time saved; mechanism=explicitly drop areas where clubs are already ahead or rules already dominate, such as concession demand forecasting, dynamic ticket pricing done by vendors, and minor-league free-agent triage; baseline=build tools by request; intervention=a short scoring rubric rating each candidate on value size, public-signal edge and backtestability.\nASSUMPTION: Ticketing and concessions forecasting at MLB clubs is already mature and vendor-supported, so a marginal internal build adds little.\nUNKNOWN: club_existing_tooling_inventory",
