    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-211812-efe1740f0c77/candidates/0/4",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-211812-efe1740f0c77",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p1-divergent",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? The current date is early October 2026, in the Cubs' offseason (their 2026 season ended in the Wild Card Series). A prior experiment found that triaging the November minor-league free-agent pool offers little: the low-attention pool holds under one next-season contributor per club per year, and a simple 'played in MLB last year' rule already finds most value. Prefer recurring decisions where the value at stake is large enough to matter, where public information available at decision time could plausibly beat a simple rule, and where a retrospective test on public history is possible. Consider front office, player development, scouting, coaching and business-of-baseball decisions, and name where software is unnecessary or clubs are already ahead. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names one problem, the decision owner, the concrete recurring decision and its timing, the size of value at stake, the mechanism of advantage, the simple baseline a club already uses, and the software intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Problem family: estimating the marginal value of coaching and player-development staff hires. This is a decision family not covered by the explored list.\nIDEA: Decision owner: the President of Baseball Operations and GM, with input from the farm director for minor-league staff.\nIDEA: Recurring decision: hiring, retaining or replacing MLB coaches, such as hitting, pitching, catching and bench coaches.\nIDEA: Timing: each October through December, and staff turnover across MLB clusters in the weeks after clubs are eliminated. Early October 2026 is inside this window for the Cubs.\nIDEA: Recurring decision: filling minor-league coordinator and affiliate coach roles each winter. These hires shape many player-seasons at once.\nIDEA: Value at stake (hypothesis): a coach touches every player on the staff or lineup for multiple seasons. A real coach effect would therefore compound across far more player-seasons than any single roster move.\nIDEA: Value at stake (hypothesis): coaching salaries are small compared with player payroll, so a good evaluation could have high leverage per dollar if true coach effects exist.\nIDEA: Simple baseline a club already uses: hire by reputation, playing career, network referrals, interviews, and the outcomes of the coach's prior team.\nIDEA: Mechanism of advantage: public team and staff directories, media transaction reports and Baseball-Reference coaching records can be joined to player-level public metrics. This builds a coach-player linkage panel.\nIDEA: Mechanism of advantage: use movement-based identification. Track the same players under different coaches, and the same coaches with different players. This separates coach effects from player aging and talent.\nIDEA: Mechanism of advantage: the model would estimate within-player changes after a coaching change, compared against matched players whose coaches did not change. It would use public outcome metrics such as chase rate, zone contact, pitch-mix shifts and walk rate.\nIDEA: Software intervention: a coach-candidate dossier generator. For each candidate it would report the before/after trajectories of players they coached, with uncertainty intervals, matched controls and sample-size warnings.\nIDEA: Software intervention: a retrospective test. Freeze data as of each past offseason, rank the coaching hires made that winter, and check whether the rankings predicted the next one to two seasons of player-change metrics better than the prior-team-outcome baseline.\nIDEA: Unconventional extension: treat staff as a scarce market. Flag undervalued coaches whose players improved but whose teams lost, since a team-record baseline would systematically overlook them.\nASSUMPTION: Coach effects on player outcomes are large enough and persistent enough to detect above noise. This is the central challenge, and the result may well be a null.\nASSUMPTION: Public staff assignment data is complete and accurately dated enough to build a linkage panel. Mid-season title changes and shared roles may blur attribution.\nASSUMPTION: Player improvement after a coaching change is not dominated by confounders that public data cannot separate. Examples include regression to the mean, front-office technology, private biomechanics programs, and the same front office hiring both the player and the coach.\nASSUMPTION: Hitting and pitching coaches control enough of player development to deserve credit. Many clubs spread instruction across analysts, coordinators and private trainers, so attribution may be meaningless.\nASSUMPTION: The number of coach moves per year gives enough independent events for a retrospective test. Small N could make any ranking statistically indistinguishable from the reputation baseline.\nASSUMPTION: Clubs that already analyze coaches internally with private data are likely ahead of a public-data model. Software may then add little beyond organizing candidate research.\nASSUMPTION: Interpersonal fit, communication and clubhouse trust may dominate a coach's value and resist public measurement. A quantitative dossier might mislead more than it informs if over-weighted.\nASSUMPTION: Software may be unnecessary if the binding constraint is candidate availability or salary competition rather than evaluation quality.\nASSUMPTION: Minor-league staff effects might be more detectable than MLB effects because the player samples are larger. However, public minor-league tracking data may be too thin or too recent to support this.\nUNKNOWN: magnitude_of_true_mlb_coach_effects\nUNKNOWN: annual_count_of_mlb_hitting_and_pitching_coach_changes\nUNKNOWN: completeness_of_public_coaching_staff_histories\nUNKNOWN: availability_of_public_minor_league_staff_assignments_by_season\nUNKNOWN: cubs_current_coaching_staff_vacancies_october_2026\nUNKNOWN: cubs_existing_internal_coach_evaluation_methods\nUNKNOWN: whether_other_clubs_already_use_coach_effect_models\nUNKNOWN: minimum_sample_needed_to_distinguish_coach_ranking_from_reputation_baseline",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
      "authority": {
